/-!
# The sheaf of ideals cutting out the zero scheme of a section

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, let `L` be an
invertible `𝒪_X`-module and let `s` be a global section of `L`.  Stacks 0845 cuts out the zero
scheme of `s` by the quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals which on an étale chart `U` is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt`, the ideal generated by the values `l(s|_U)`
of the linear functionals on `L(U)`.  This file builds that sheaf of ideals and shows it is
quasi-coherent, so that the surjectivity half of Stacks 03MB produces a closed subspace of `X`
whose defining ideal is the evaluation ideal on every affine étale chart.

That conclusion —
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_isInvertible` — is
the hypothesis `hZ` which every assertion of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeProperties.lean` carries, so with it those
assertions become unconditional.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeIdealAt` is the ideal of regular functions on an étale chart
  whose restriction to every affine étale chart over it lies in the evaluation ideal there, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeIdealSheaf` is the resulting sheaf of ideals of `𝒪_X`.

## Main results

* `Ideal.mem_map_span_range_dual`: **dual base change for a finitely presented module.**  A
  functional on `C ⊗[A] M` takes `1 ⊗ x` into the extension to `C` of the ideal of `A` generated
  by the values at `x` of the `A`-functionals on `M`, when `M` is finitely presented and `C` is
  flat over `A`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_le_map`: consequently the evaluation ideal
  over the smaller of two affine étale charts is generated by the restrictions of the evaluation
  ideal over the larger one.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_sieve`: **membership in the evaluation
  ideal over an affine chart is local**, by faithfully flat descent along a finite affine
  refinement of the covering.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt`: on an affine étale
  chart the sheaf of ideals built here *is* the evaluation ideal.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_zeroSchemeIdealSheaf`: it is quasi-coherent.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_isCartesian` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_isInvertible`:
  **a closed subspace of `X` has the evaluation ideal of `s` as its defining ideal on every
  affine étale chart.**

## Implementation notes

The three inputs that were missing when
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeQuasicoherentIdeal.lean` was written are all
available now and all three are used.

* Descent, Proposition 03DX(2) is unconditional,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isCartesianOnAffineCharts_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`, so an invertible
  `L` — quasi-coherent by `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasicoherentOnSite_of_isInvertible` — is
  cartesian on affine charts.  This is what makes the evaluation ideals restrict into one another.
* An invertible module on the small étale site of an algebraic space is finite locally free of
  rank one (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isFiniteLocallyFreeOfRank_one_of_isInvertible`), hence of
  finite presentation, so its sections over an affine chart are a finitely presented module by
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.module_finitePresentation_sections_of_isFinitePresentationOnSite`.
  Finite presentation is exactly what makes the *dual* base change, and with it the reverse
  containment of evaluation ideals, available.
* The surjectivity half of Stacks 03MB,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealSheaf_eq_of_isQuasicoherentOnSite`, turns the
  quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals into a closed subspace.

The sheaf of ideals is not the sheafification of the chartwise evaluation ideals but the family
of functions whose restriction to *every* affine chart below lies in the evaluation ideal there.
That family is visibly stable under restriction, its membership condition is local by the descent
below, and on an affine chart it is the evaluation ideal itself, so nothing is lost; the
advantage is that no sheafification has to be computed.  By
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.eq_sectionEvaluationIdeal_of_forall_isAffine` it agrees with
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdeal`.

